> It's been a while, but IIRC, in steady-state we were mostly just keeping a constant lag, rather than falling further behind. However, periodically we'd get unlucky and we'd fall behind by an extra segment due to a load spike or something, so over a period of hours the lag would keep getting slowly worse in a series of small step changes. Does that make sense?

:thinking: I can see how this would happen, without the change from https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/pull/13583 we'd be waiting for the new segment to be created, at which point we'd try to read and send the whole segment. IIRC the max size for a segment is 128MB. That would explain the consistent state of always being a segment or so behind. If there was sufficient latency for a period during which we're trying to read and send that whole segment, and your scrape throughput is also sufficiently high enough to also mean a new full segment was written to disk during that time, you could potentially fall multiple segments behind over a long period of time.

@djudd have you not noticed any large chunks of missing data since upgrading post https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/pull/13583 ? That essentially introduced a bug where Prometheus would "catch up" by actually dropping all samples between `current segment RW is reading` and `newest segment` when it notices it's behind.
